(Bug #87: a buffer stored in a `value` kept the buffer's STRUCT pointer as
 its payload while the tag beside it said text, so every read dereferenced
 the 24-byte header and printed the capacity field's low byte - `@` for a
 64-byte buffer, an empty line for a dynamic one whose low byte is 0. That
 is bug #51's defect in the `value` positions #51's fix did not reach, and
 the owner's #51 ruling settles it the same way: the cast-free spelling
 means what `as text` means.

 The two declared sizes are load-bearing. 64 and 65 differ ONLY in the
 capacity byte, so an answer read out of the header tracks them (`@`, then
 `A`) while the right answer does not move.)

a buffer called sized is 64 bytes in size.
append "first" to sized.
a value called carried is sized.
Print carried.

a buffer called roomier is 65 bytes in size.
append "first" to roomier.
a value called held is roomier.
Print held.

(The register's headline repro: a dynamic buffer, whose capacity's low byte
 is 0, so the wrong answer was a silent empty line rather than a character.)
a buffer called made is "ABC".
a value called echoed is made.
Print echoed.
Print "{echoed}".

(The neighbours that were always right, and must stay right.)
a text called direct is made.
Print direct.
Print "{made}".
a value called viacast is made as text.
Print viacast.

(An empty buffer must give empty text, not a header read.)
a buffer called untouched is 32 bytes in size.
a value called blank is untouched.
Print "[{blank}]".

(The `value` says it holds text - it did so before the fix too, which is
 what made the wrong payload a self-describing lie rather than undefined
 territory. Both answers must still agree with the bytes now delivered.)
Print carried's type.
If carried is a text then, Print "predicate: text". Otherwise, Print "predicate: not text".

(And a text read back out of the `value` carries the same bytes.)
a text called back is echoed.
Print "back=[{back}]".
